 she remembers since she the  first author of this of this piece about  our meeting getting and so she starts  something that she calls meeting  doomsday with a with a small group um at  Asana marketing just nine people and  what she has them do and their volume  volunteers is uh that they went into  their calendars and for 48 hours they  removed all small standing meetings so  five or fewer people and then the idea  was they kind of stopped and they sort  of thought about the meetings and then  and then we're back in her team they  calculated how much time people saved it  was just sort of amazing it was 11 hours  per month just just like think about  that's a lot of time and there's just  one woman she's the star of this  Francesca who works in London so she  saved 32 hours a month so and this is  just by sort of pausing to think okay so  moving ahead so um what um what Rebecca  LED next was something that you called  meeting reset and this was for a larger  group and what she did was uh she she  had like 60 people and some of them did  sort of the heavy version this was where  they actually removed all standing  meetings just like um in in doomsday she  called it full doomsday and they  remember move the standing meetings for  48 hours and and they assessed actually  there's a little bit more rigor in this  one uh and I was involved in this a  little bit is what we had him do is  assess both the value of the meeting on  a three-point scale each each meeting  that they'd removed and also  um how um how much work or how much  trouble it was and then they made  decisions about removing meetings and  and and on average you can see that  people in the full participation group  saved about five hours and the people in  the light about three hours so sort of  moving ahead  um let's so let's have the next slide  let's talk a little bit about the  results and this is from the meeting  Playbook and so I'm not going to read  all of the details but you can see and  this is just an incredible number to me  that um and this isn't like a  well-managed group 48 of meetings were  not considered high value why the people  once they assess them and you can see  the 265 hours saved per month the next  one is really important from a matter of  redesign in something that we're really  thinking about a lot now which is that  70 percent of the time savings came from  not getting rid of meetings but from  redesigning meetings so for example  making a 30-minute meetings 25 minutes  one thing that I would suggest that you  might try doing and there's some  companies I work where they do this is  see if you in your team can change say  30-minute meetings to a default 25  minute meetings or 60 minutes to a  default 10-minute meetings there's  actually some pretty good research that  when you do that  um that your your level of Engagement  and and uh and and your is higher in  your level of stress is lower in the  next meeting so that's one of the kind  of things they did and then the other  thing which which you know the I the old  notion of that was a meeting that could  have been an email is that they also  would add an asynchronous component so  just for example people and and I'm not  sure this happened at Asana but it  happened in other companies I work with  where what they'll do is they'll they'll  change the Rhythm to say well we'll meet  every two weeks but we'll have an email  update every week so and oh and and then  the other thing is is that uh we were  working with this great Stanford  undergraduate Stephen Lai who did a  Model to sort of figure out what the  most valuable and least valuable  meetings were and one of the Hallmarks  and this is just so obvious it's  ridiculous  um but but a lot of this stuff is just  common sense is is that the meetings  that didn't have an agenda  um or didn't have a clear name or  purpose those were the ones that tended  to be least value and and that that old  notion that I'm not going to go to a  meeti